{:edb/db-before-t 1644 :edb/db-after-t 1645 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534957 50 #inst "2026-10-08T12:04:54.691Z" 13194139534957 true] [17592186155373 1104 #edb/ref 17592186155377 13194139534957 true] [17592186155379 1104 #edb/ref 17592186155381 13194139534957 true] [17592186155385 1104 #edb/ref 17592186155390 13194139534957 true] [17592186155397 1104 #edb/ref 17592186155399 13194139534957 true] [17592186155427 1104 #edb/ref 17592186155432 13194139534957 true] [17592186155433 1104 #edb/ref 17592186155435 13194139534957 true] [17592186155438 1104 #edb/ref 17592186155440 13194139534957 true] [17592186156984 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 6y = (3x+1)\\sin(x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, what is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_p(x)?$" 13194139534957 true] [17592186156984 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 6y = (3x+1)\\sin(x)$.\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, what is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_p(x)$?" 13194139534957 false] [17592186156984 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p$, we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." 13194139534957 false] [17592186156984 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." 13194139534957 true] [17592186156985 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 5y = \\cos 2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/ae29bceba4d6a1f250f244b26583abeb4b8c2fbe33d380dfa4332b051bb21078.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$ ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/c6725d6cd732c1ca7f21ab9a8dac245bc396c04a623cfb8e2eda31751ddaa89e.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/1d1c8a545fb741413f7cdd2f6f3fa6190fbdda74290e206cc4a7f8061f97ff53.png)." 13194139534957 true] [17592186156985 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 5y = \\cos 2x$.\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u$, we have $u(x) =$ $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$ $AAAAA$." 13194139534957 false] [17592186156985 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 5y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p$, we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u$, we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(ue^{5x})^{′} - 5(ue^{5x}) &= \\cos 2x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{5x} + 5ue^{5x} - 5ue^{5x} &= \\cos 2x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{5x} + 5ue^{5x} - 5ue^{5x} &= \\cos 2x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{5x} &= \\cos 2x \\\\\nu^{′} &= e^{-5x}\\cos 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{′} &= e^{-5x}\\cos 2x \\\\\n∫u^{′}dx &= ∫e^{-5x}\\cos 2xdx \\\\\nu &= ∫e^{-5x}\\cos 2xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\ny_p = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p} &= u(x)e^{5x} \\\\\n&= ∫e^{-5x}\\cos 2xdx \\cdot e^{5x} \\\\\n&= e^{5x}∫e^{-5x}\\cos 2xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534957 false] [17592186156985 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 5y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{5x} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534957 true] [17592186156986 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 3y = 12\\sinh 3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/ae29bceba4d6a1f250f244b26583abeb4b8c2fbe33d380dfa4332b051bb21078.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/c6725d6cd732c1ca7f21ab9a8dac245bc396c04a623cfb8e2eda31751ddaa89e.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/1d1c8a545fb741413f7cdd2f6f3fa6190fbdda74290e206cc4a7f8061f97ff53.png)." 13194139534957 true] [17592186156986 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 3y = 12\\sinh 3x$.\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u$, we have $u(x) =$ $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$ $AAAAA$." 13194139534957 false] [17592186156986 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 3y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p$, we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u$, we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(ue^{3x})^{′} - 3(ue^{3x}) &= 12\\sinh 3x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{3x} + 3ue^{3x} - 3ue^{3x} &= 12\\sinh 3x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{3x} + 3ue^{3x} - 3ue^{3x} &= 12\\sinh 3x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{3x} &= 12\\sinh 3x \\\\\nu^{′} &= 12e^{-3x}\\sinh 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sinh 3x = \\dfrac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{′} &= 12e^{-3x}\\sinh 3x \\\\\n&= 12e^{-3x}(\\frac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\\n&= 6e^{-3x}(e^{3x} - e^{-3x}) \\\\\n&= 6 - 6e^{-6x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{′} &= 6 - 6e^{-6x} \\\\\n∫u^{′}dx &= ∫6 - 6e^{-6x}dx \\\\\nu &= 6x + e^{-6x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\ny_p = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p} &= u(x)e^{3x} \\\\\n&= 6xe^{3x} + e^{-3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534957 false] [17592186156986 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 3y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\sinh 3x = \\dfrac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =12{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =6-6{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6-6{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6-6{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =6x+{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =6x{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534957 true] [17592186157386 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322449 13194139534957 true] [17592186157386 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322456 13194139534957 true] [17592186157386 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322463 13194139534957 true] [17592186157386 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322470 13194139534957 true] [17592186157386 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322477 13194139534957 true] [17592186157387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322556 13194139534957 true] [17592186157387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322575 13194139534957 true] [17592186157387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322594 13194139534957 true] [17592186157388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322484 13194139534957 true] [17592186157388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322503 13194139534957 true] [17592186157388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322522 13194139534957 true] [17592186157388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322539 13194139534957 true] [17592186157695 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534957 true] [17592186157695 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139534957 true] [17592186157695 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 4y = e^{2x}$.\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nAccording to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\nSolving for $u$, we have $u(x) =$ {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $y_{p}(x) =$ {{field-3}}." 13194139534957 false] [17592186157695 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-4x})}^{′}+4(u{e}^{-4x}) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x}+4u{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{6x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534957 true] [17592186157696 1017 #edb/ref 17592186155391 13194139534957 false] [17592186157696 1017 #edb/ref 17592186155403 13194139534957 false] [17592186157696 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322439 13194139534957 true] [17592186157696 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322444 13194139534957 true] [17592186157696 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534957 true] [17592186157696 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139534957 true] [17592186157696 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} - 2y = \\sin 4x$.\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nAccording to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\nSolving for $u$, we have $u(x) =$ {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $y_{p}(x) =$ {{field-3}}." 13194139534957 false] [17592186157696 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534957 true] [17592186157698 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534957 true] [17592186157698 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139534957 true] [17592186157698 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} - 2y = 4\\cosh 2x$.\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nAccording to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\nSolving for $u$, we have $u(x) =$ {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $y_{p}(x) =$ {{field-3}}." 13194139534957 false] [17592186157698 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =4{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =4{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}({e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =2+2{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =2+2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2+2{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =2x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534957 true] [17592186322439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322440 13194139534957 true] [17592186322439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322441 13194139534957 true] [17592186322439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322442 13194139534957 true] [17592186322439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322443 13194139534957 true] [17592186322439 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139534957 true] [17592186322439 1041 "field-2" 13194139534957 true] [17592186322439 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322440 13194139534957 true] [17592186322439 1110 #uuid "bc1b58f9-6fec-5045-a2c7-9a63cd9e58e0" 13194139534957 true] [17592186322440 1034 "\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534957 true] [17592186322440 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534957 true] [17592186322440 1111 #uuid "886f45b3-41db-5a5c-9653-b06f1a3dd432" 13194139534957 true] [17592186322441 1034 "\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534957 true] [17592186322441 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534957 true] [17592186322441 1111 #uuid "6be492da-4423-5ee0-b031-0265fdc21c45" 13194139534957 true] [17592186322442 1034 "\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534957 true] [175921