mplementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-2x})}^{′}+2(u{e}^{-2x}) & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2u{e}^{-2x}+2u{e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}} & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-2{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534957 true] [17592186322576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322577 13194139534957 true] [17592186322576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322578 13194139534957 true] [17592186322576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322579 13194139534957 true] [17592186322576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322580 13194139534957 true] [17592186322576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322581 13194139534957 true] [17592186322576 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139534957 true] [17592186322576 1041 "field-1" 13194139534957 true] [17592186322576 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322578 13194139534957 true] [17592186322576 1110 #uuid "dd35f3d0-9ee3-5e5c-994a-2631788763d4" 13194139534957 true] [17592186322577 1034 "u(x){e}^{-5x}" 13194139534957 true